1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Start with a set of three elements. Every relation on it is built from pairs of those elements.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:13,000
There are nine pairs in all, one for each cell of this three by three grid.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:21,000
A relation is any choice of cells. Here it is one, two, one-two, two-three: four cells chosen out of nine.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Each of the nine cells is in or out, so there are two to the nine, which is 512, relations on a three-element set.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Write the same relation as a matrix: row one, column two is a 1 because 1 is related to 2.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Or draw it as arrows between the elements. An arrow from 2 to 3 is the same fact as the 1 in that cell.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Two features of the matrix carry every property in this lesson: the diagonal, and the mirror pair of cells.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
So there are three diagonal cells and three mirror pairs. Six independent decisions, not nine, once you know what you want.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Reflexive means every element is related to itself: every diagonal cell is a 1.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Irreflexive means the opposite: no element is related to itself, so every diagonal cell is a 0.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The identity relation is reflexive, the empty relation is not, and the relation below is neither.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Count them: three cells forced to 1, six free, so 2 to the 6, which is 64 reflexive relations.

13
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Symmetric: if 1 is related to 2 then 2 is related to 1. In the matrix, the two mirrored cells must agree.

14
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Here the pair is broken: the 1 from 1 to 3 has no partner. One missing mirror is enough to fail.

15
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Count them: three diagonal cells are free, and each of the three mirror pairs is one decision, so 2 to the 6 again.

16
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Symmetric relations are exactly the ones equal to their own inverse. That is a useful one-line test.

17
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Antisymmetric: two different elements may not point at each other. At most one of each mirror pair is a 1.

18
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Divisibility is the standard example: 1 divides 2 and 2 does not divide 1, so the pair is allowed.

19
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:28,000
The identity relation is symmetric and antisymmetric at the same time, so the two are not opposites.

20
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Count them: three diagonal cells free, three mirror pairs with three choices each, so 8 times 27, which is 216.

21
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Transitive: a two-step path must come with a one-step shortcut. One to two and two to three forces one to three.

22
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Here the shortcut is missing, so the relation is not transitive. Adding that one cell repairs it.

23
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Now the popular claim: if a relation is symmetric and transitive, must it be reflexive? No.

24
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:12,000
The smallest counterexample is one self-loop on a two-element set: symmetric, transitive, not reflexive.

25
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:20,000
The transitive closure of R is R plus every shortcut its paths force. For this relation that adds one cell.

26
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Repeat until nothing new appears: with a longer chain, one to two, two to three, three to four, three cells arrive.

27
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:36,000
The reflexive closure fills the diagonal, the symmetric closure mirrors every cell. Each is the smallest fix of its own defect.

28
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Smallest is checkable: every transitive relation containing R also contains the closure. That was tested by listing them.

29
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:52,000
An equivalence relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive at once. Same parity is the familiar example.

30
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:00,000
The equivalence class of an element is everything related to it. Here the classes are the odds and the evens.

31
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Classes are non-empty, never overlap, and together cover the set: that is a partition, and it is forced, not chosen.

32
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
So equivalence relations on a set are partitions of it. On three elements there are five of each, counted here.

33
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Every count in this table comes from one idea: which cells are forced and which are free.

34
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:32,000
For four elements: 65536 relations in all, 4096 reflexive, 1024 symmetric, 11664 antisymmetric, 16 both.

35
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Transitive has no such formula. The counts are 2, 13, 171, 3994, and beyond four elements they are only ever computed.

36
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Equivalence relations on three elements: five. On four: fifteen. Same numbers as the partitions, because they are the same objects.

37
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,000
One: read the diagonal for reflexive and irreflexive, the mirror pairs for symmetric and antisymmetric.

38
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Two: antisymmetric is not the negation of symmetric. Both, neither, and exactly one are all possible.

39
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Three: one missing pair breaks a universal property, and one element with no arrow breaks the claim that symmetry plus transitivity gives reflexivity.

